@page "/form-file"

<CatHead Title="File inputs">
    A picker that can be styled, and a dropzone that takes a real drop.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="Picking files">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormFile.FileInput" Title="File input" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>&lt;input type="file"&gt;</code> cannot be restyled: its button is a shadow-DOM part with
            only <code>::file-selector-button</code> exposed, and its &ldquo;No file chosen&rdquo; text is
            not addressable at all. <code>.form-file</code> hides the input behind a label styled as a
            button, which keeps the real control — a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> for a file input opens the
            picker on click <em>and</em> on <kbd>Enter</kbd>, with no JavaScript and no ARIA.
        </p>
        <p>
            The input is clipped rather than <code>display: none</code>, so it stays focusable and in the
            accessibility tree. The focus ring is drawn on the label, because the focused element is the
            one the reader cannot see.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>::file-selector-button</code> is styled too, for anyone using the input bare.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormFile.DropzoneFileList" Title="Dropzone and file list" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live — drag a file onto it.</strong> A drop target that is <em>also</em> a click
            target: drag and drop alone is unusable by keyboard and awkward on touch, so the zone wraps a
            real file input and the drag handling is an enhancement on top.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>data-dropzone</code> hands it to <code>sednaUi.dropzone</code>, which maintains
            <code>.dropzone--over</code> and puts a dropped file into the zone's own input as a
            <code>change</code> event — so an existing handler, Blazor's <code>InputFile</code> included,
            sees a dropped file exactly as it sees a chosen one. Leave the attribute off to own the drag
            handling yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
            Two things it does that are easy to get wrong alone: <code>dragleave</code> fires when the
            pointer moves onto a <em>child</em>, so a naive enter/leave pair flickers and then sticks as
            soon as the zone contains an icon and a label. And <code>dragover</code> must have its default
            prevented, or the browser refuses the drop and <em>navigates to the file</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The file list is a real <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, so the count is announced. Sizes are
            monospace so a column of them lines up. <code>.file-item--error</code> carries the icon as
            well as the colour.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="States and limits">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormFile.DropzoneStates" Title="Idle, dragged over and rejected" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Idle, dragged-over, and rejected. <code>.dropzone--over</code> is set by the script and has no
            CSS pseudo-class equivalent, so it is the one state an app cannot express in markup alone.
        </p>
        <p>
            Rejection is <code>aria-invalid</code> on the zone plus a <code>.form-error</code> naming the
            file and the limit. Set the attribute rather than a class: it is what a screen reader
            announces, so the two cannot disagree. &ldquo;Invalid file&rdquo; is not a message — say which
            file and why.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormFile.Limits" Title="Limits and a refused file" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Say the limits before the reader picks: how many files, which types and how large, as a
            <code>.form-hint</code> inside the zone that the input names in <code>aria-describedby</code>.
            Put the types in <code>accept</code> too, so the picker offers only those.
        </p>
        <p>
            A file the app turns away stays in the list as a <code>.file-item--error</code>, with the reason
            in its <code>.file-meta</code> &mdash; &ldquo;24 MB is over the 10 MB limit&rdquo;, not
            &ldquo;invalid file&rdquo; &mdash; and a <code>.form-error</code> under the zone says how many were
            not added. The checks are the app's; nothing here reads a file.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Sizes and places">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormFile.PageDrop" Title="Across the whole page" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; drag a file anywhere over this page.</strong> Write the overlay once, as a
            <code>.dropzone.dropzone--page</code> with <code>data-dropzone="page"</code>, and name the input that
            takes the drop with <code>data-dropzone-input</code> &mdash; without it, the overlay's own
            <code>input[type=file]</code>. While files are dragged over the window the script sets
            <code>.dropzone--over</code> on it, which is what shows it; leaving the window, dropping, ending the
            drag and <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> take it away. A drag that carries no files never shows it.
        </p>
        <p>
            A drop anywhere reaches the input as a <code>change</code> event, as an ordinary zone's does, so
            the same handler serves the picker and the page. The overlay takes no pointer: an ordinary zone
            under it still lights up and keeps its own drop. It is <code>aria-hidden</code>, because the
            picker is the way in without a pointer.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormFile.DropzoneLarge" Title="Large, as the main action" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.dropzone--lg</code> is a zone that is the reason the page exists &mdash; an import, a
            first upload &mdash; with more room and a larger glyph. Everything else about it is the same
            zone.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormFile.DropzoneCompact" Title="Compact, under a composer" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.dropzone--sm</code> lays the zone out as one row at <code>.btn--sm</code>'s height, for a
            reply or comment box where a full-height panel would outweigh the text field it serves. The
            input, <code>data-dropzone</code> and every state are the same zone's.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.FormFile.AttachmentField" Title="Inside a form" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            The whole field in place: a <code>&lt;fieldset class="form-section"&gt;</code> so the legend is
            announced with every control inside it, the zone, the list of what is attached, and the
            <a href="/form"><code>.form-actions</code></a> row the form ends with.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>.form-label--required</code> is decoration. Put <code>required</code> on the input, which
            is what is announced and enforced.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>
